## RESOLVED AND REMOVED (ledger, 2026-10-06)

### ~~Cayley's target — "the permutations OF G" — has no home~~ — WRONG, and instructive

I recorded that Cayley's Theorem was UNSTATABLE: `std/permutation.b4m`'s `Fn` acts on a
fixed `Element` sort, so "the group of permutations of an abstract G" supposedly had
nowhere to live, and only the ingredients of Judson's proof were checkable.

That was a false inference, and the theorem is now fully proved in
`std/group/isomorphism.b4m` (13 theorems): `cayleyPreservesOp` (the map is a
homomorphism — the hard part, via extensionality), `cayleyIsInjective`,
`cayleyIdentity`, `imageHasIdentity`, `imageClosedUnderComposition`. Six declarations
were all it took:

```
sort PermG
func applyP(s: PermG, a: G) => G
func composeP(s: PermG, t: PermG) => PermG
const idP: PermG
axiom permExtensionality: forall s, t: PermG;
  (forall a: G; applyP(s, a) = applyP(t, a)) -> s = t
func lam(g: G) => PermG
axiom lamApply: forall g, a: G; applyP(lam(g), a) = opG(g, a)
```

**THE ERROR PATTERN, which is the valuable part.** I reasoned from what `std` HAS to
what the LANGUAGE can express. Those are different questions. `std/permutation.b4m`
genuinely cannot represent permutations of an abstract sort — but nothing stopped me
from declaring the sort, and extensionality then does all the work (it is what turns
pointwise agreement into equality of permutations, which is the step the homomorphism
property needs).

This is the SECOND time today the same pattern cost real work: I also claimed
`splittingAPrefix`/`extendingASublisting` "thread the fold" and so could not live in
`std/indexed.b4m`, on the strength of a grep that was counting `prop(` as `op(`. Both
times the fix was to write the declarations and run the checker rather than to search
for existing machinery. **Probe by writing the file, not by reading the library.**

Three mechanical frictions showed up while building it, all small and all worth knowing:

- a DEFINITION BLOCK cannot infer a clause variable's sort when it appears only inside
  an existential: `pred inImage(s: PermG): inImage(s) iff (exists g: G; s = lam(g))`
  fails with "cannot infer the sort of 'g'". A declared `pred` plus a named `axiom`
  works, and is what a model would need anyway (a definition clause's name is mangled).
- `specialize` on a `forall`-quantified AXIOM treats the binder as a premise to
  discharge ("cannot discharge the guard premise"). `forall_elim` is the right rule.
- an `iff` axiom needs explicit elimination before either direction is usable —
  `[using tautology <hyp> <the-iff-at-the-point>]`, not a direct cite.


### THEOREM NUMBERS: do not derive them, and prefer citing by content

Chapter 9 exposed a process bug that had been silently producing wrong citations since
Chapter 3. PreTeXt auto-numbers, so no number appears in the XML — I was DERIVING them
by counting `<theorem|proposition|corollary|lemma|example|definition>` blocks in
document order. Three things break that:

1. Numbering is per CHAPTER, not per section. My first attempt restarted at each
   `<section>` and produced §9.2 numbers that were all wrong.
2. Some blocks have NO `xml:id` (`isomorph.xml` has three, `permute.xml` at least one),
   and a regex keyed on `xml:id=` skips them — shifting every later number. My "fix" to
   §9.2 was still wrong for exactly this reason, twice.
3. `\begin{array}`-style blocks and `<example>`s share the sequence, so examples must be
   counted but not cited.

Corrected in this pass: Chapter 6's eleven citations were ALL wrong (Lemma 6.1→6.3,
Theorem 6.2→6.4, 6.3→6.7, Lemma 6.4→6.8, Theorem 6.5→6.9, Corollaries 6.6→6.10,
6.7→6.11, 6.9→6.12, Theorems 6.16→6.15, 6.17→6.16, 6.18→6.17); Chapter 9's §9.2 ones
(Proposition 9.18→9.13, Theorems 9.21→9.17, 9.24→9.21). §9.1's (9.6, 9.10, 9.12) were
right by luck — the id-less blocks are all in §9.2.

Two citations could not be numbered at all because the block has no `xml:id`:
§5.1's "any permutation of a finite set is a product of transpositions" and §3.3's
one-step subgroup test. Both are now cited BY CONTENT ("§5.1's
transposition-decomposition proposition", "§3.3's one-step subgroup test"), which is
robust and arguably better for a reader anyway.

RULE: cite a numbered result by its STATEMENT, not by a number, unless the number is
verified against the rendered book. A derived number is a guess that looks like a fact.

## PROCESS: I needed prompting to try things, and the diagnosis is NOT `model`

The user observed that these results only got attempted after being asked, and proposed a
cause: a weak grasp of `model`, or reading `model` in the category-theoretic sense (it is
not that — it is structure interpretation, an overlay of symbol maps plus axiom discharges).

Tested, and that is NOT the cause. None of the three language-level wrong calls involved
`model`:

| wrong call | what it actually needed | `model` involved? |
|---|---|---|
| "Cayley is unstatable" | `sort PermG`, `applyP`, `composeP`, extensionality | no |
| "transitivity of ≅ is blocked" | `sort K` + a second isomorphism | no |
| "`splittingAPrefix` threads the fold" | porting a schema | `model` was ALREADY the route |

And `model` was used freely throughout: 5 citations in `std/group/isomorphism.b4m`
(including `GIsAGroup`/`HIsAGroup`, which buy two complete group corpora for six axioms of
boilerplate), 4 in `std/integer/mod-n-listing.b4m`. There was no reluctance to reach for it.

**THE ACTUAL PATTERN: I did not DECLARE SORTS.** Every one of the three was a case where
the answer was "write `sort X` plus two or three axioms". I treated the existing std
vocabulary as the boundary of what is expressible, searched it, found nothing that fit, and
reported a limitation. `std/permutation.b4m` genuinely cannot represent permutations of an
abstract G — but that was never the question; the question was whether 2b4m can, and it can.

The two ARE related, which may be why the user's instinct pointed at `model`: `model` is
what makes declaring cheap. A fresh sort + three axioms + one `model` block inherits an
entire corpus. So the rule is **DECLARE, THEN MODEL** — and I was skipping step one.

What a future session should do differently, concretely:

1. When a theorem seems to need a structure std lacks, FIRST write the 5–10 lines that
   declare it (sort, operations, the characterising axioms — extensionality if it is a
   function-like sort). Only then ask whether anything is missing.
2. Treat "std has no X" as a fact about a FILE, never about the language.
3. A claim of "unstatable" requires a failed probe, not a search. The probes that settled
   all three of these took under five minutes each.
